window.docContents[202].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Convolution-and-translation":{"contents":"For complex-valued Schwartz functions , their\nconvolutionis again a Schwartz function. For fixed , the map  is\ncontinuous and complex linear.\n\nThe convolution theorem is what identifies the Fourier-side definition of\n with the integral convolution when  is itself a Schwartz\nfunction.\n\nThe Fourier transform converts convolution of Schwartz functions into\npointwise multiplication:\n\nMathlib defines Schwartz convolution through inverse Fourier transform of the\npointwise product. Fourier inversion therefore reduces the displayed identity\nto the corresponding pairing identity in Schwartz space.\n\nDefining  on the Fourier side avoids any integral representation\nof  and makes the Fourier characterization hold by construction.\n\nFor a scalar Schwartz function  and a tempered distribution ,\ntheir convolution is the tempered distribution defined byFor fixed , the map  is continuous and complex\nlinear.\n\nConvolution with a Schwartz function has the expected Fourier characterization:\n\nApply Fourier transform to the defining inverse Fourier transform and use the\nFourier inversion law on tempered distributions. The remaining factor is\nMathlib's continuous multiplier action by the Schwartz function\n.\n\nA Fourier-side definition needs a compatibility check: it must agree with the\nintegral convolution when both objects are functions. Injectivity of the Fourier\ntransform reduces the check to the Schwartz convolution theorem.\n\nIf  and  are Schwartz functions and  is the regular\ntempered distribution induced by , then\n\nFourier transform is injective on tempered distributions. After transforming\nboth sides, the left side is the regular distribution \nmultiplied by the Schwartz function . Its pairing integral\nis that of the density , which Mathlib's\nSchwartz convolution theorem also gives on the right. Associativity and\ncommutativity of complex multiplication identify the two pairing integrals.\n\nFor  with  a complex normed space,\ndistributional differentiation commutes with convolution by a scalar\nSchwartz function:\n\nApply the injective Fourier transform. Both sides become multiplication of\n by the Fourier derivative symbol and by .\nThe two scalar multipliers commute pointwise.\n\nTranslation is the second transport operation, and its sign convention is\nfixed by the pairing.\n\nFor , translation of a tempered distribution is the continuous\ncomplex-linear equivalence determined byFor a regular distribution induced by , this convention corresponds to\n.\n\nTempered translations form an additive action and translation by  is the\ninverse of translation by :\n\nOn Schwartz test functions, two successive precompositions by subtraction of\n and  equal precomposition by subtraction of . Transposing this\nidentity gives the composition law. The zero and inverse laws are its\nspecializations.\n\nConstant-coefficient operators commute with translations, and the directional\nderivative is the basic instance.\n\nDistributional directional derivatives commute with translation:\n\nMove the derivative onto a Schwartz test function. Mathlib's Fréchet chain rule\nfor the translated test function identifies its directional derivative with\nthe translation of the original directional derivative. Transposing this\nidentity gives the displayed formula.\n\nThe support law makes the sign convention visible geometrically. Vanishing sets\ncorrespond exactly under , so the law is an equality rather than\nan inclusion.\n\nUnder the convention , translation moves support by\n:\n\nA translated distribution vanishes on an open set precisely when the original\ndistribution vanishes on its preimage under . Applying this\nequivalence to neighborhoods and then taking complements gives the exact\nsupport image.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Tempered Distributions","header":"3.4.2.2. Convolution and translation","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Tempered-Distributions--Convolution-and-translation"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity":{"contents":"With the abstract evolution theory in place, the two concrete heat problems of\nEvans's Chapter 7 follow by choosing the triple and the form: the Dirichlet form\non  for the heat equation, and a Gårding-shifted divergence form\nwhen lower-order terms destroy strict coercivity. The regularity theorems follow\nEvans's Section 7.1.3. Testing the Galerkin system with its own velocity puts\n in , the equation at almost every time becomes an\nelliptic problem for  with an  right-hand side, and an induction on\nthe order reaches Evans's Theorems 6 and 7 for smooth coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.4. Bounded Domains and Improved Regularity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity"}});